/-
Fmt/M1Theirs.lean: the public data of the first toy (a product test) as the programs write them, and
four values that the programs print, recomputed in Lean. "Theirs": the programs' own data of the
first toy.

The first toy is a small test protocol that the programs (the C++ implementation that comes with
this Lean code) run at two sets of parameters, called full parameters and tiny parameters here. Its
claim is that three rows a, b, c of one oracle message have a_i b_i = c_i for every i (the heads of
Fmt/M1Types.lean and Fmt/M1Psc.lean). For each set of parameters, this file holds the parameter
bytes and the public file as the programs wrote them. Lean reads the bytes, reads the sizes off
them, spells the same bytes again from the sizes and the public file, and computes four values that
the programs print. The parameter bytes are the string that is hashed into the first value of the
hash chain from which a run's challenges are drawn; they hold the plan (the table of the protocol's
rounds), the digest of the public file and four further items (`Params`, Fmt/ParamsRead.lean). The
sizes (`Par`, Fmt/M1Types.lean) are the toy's parameters. To spell a value is to write it as the
bytes that the format fixes for it.

Sources.
  The bytes and values are put in by a program from the files as they came. They are the programs'
    own; made-up toy data only.
  They came in the programs' archive of files (sha256
    f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1; the archive is not in this
    repository):
      honest-and-altered/product/product.parameters, .configuration, .digests.tsv (full parameters);
                                 tiny.parameters, .configuration, .digests.tsv (tiny parameters).
    By the archive's README its files were made by a script of the programs' tests; that script is
    not in this repository.
  The layout of the parameter bytes is that of the format page (docs/format/FORMAT.md, which comes
  with the
    programs; here in an edition that is not the one that ships); this file was first made against a
    second edition, also not the one that ships (a stage of a proximity test is four words, its
    choices one). Of the rules that it uses, R4.4, R4.5, R4.9, R12.4, R12.5 and R12.7 are the same
    in these two editions, and R4.3 and R4.6 differ only by some sentences (a pointer; the roots of
    stored tables, of which this toy has none): compared by a program, rule by rule.
  The sizes at tiny parameters are typed into the generating program as the answers to questions
    about the programs gave them; a test compares them with what is read off the bytes.
  A second archive holds the same runs as made by another version of the programs
    (archive sha256 85ea235edeb9c30a07ded179f054662282d878325c63ec851b43ec451faaa903); public files,
    parameter bytes, plans and codes are byte for byte the same in both archives (compared by a
    program); the provers' random coins differ, and with them the proofs. That is why one string
    can be spoken of at both these editions of the format page.
  No Mathlib is imported.

1. What the file contains.
   `Theirs.full`, `Theirs.tiny`: the parameter bytes at full and at tiny parameters.
   `Theirs.pubFull`, `Theirs.pubTiny`: the one public file of each.
   `Theirs.tinyPar'`: the sizes at tiny parameters.

2. What is checked when the file compiles (tests, #guard).
   Every part of `Fmt.M1.check` (Fmt/M1ParOf.lean) holds at both strings: they are read; spelled
     again they give the same bytes; the sizes are read; the sizes fit from stage to stage; the plan
     that was read is `planOf` of these sizes, word for word and name for name; the four items have
     their names; codes, notice, stages and choices are Lean's spellings (`itemsOf`) at these sizes.
   The sizes that are read are `toyPar` of Fmt/M1Plan.lean (full) and `Theirs.tinyPar'` (tiny).
   The programs' parameter bytes are spelled by Lean from the sizes and the public file alone, byte
     for byte, at both sets of parameters.
   The configuration's digest of the public file (FORMAT.md, R4.9), the parameters' digest (R4.4)
     and, with the programs' random bytes, the label 1 and the empty statement, the run's value
     (R4.5) and the chain's first value (R4.6) are as the programs print them, at both sets of
     parameters.
   The two public files are spelled by Lean from the sizes, the first line and the target's bits
     (R12.7).

3. Theorems. No theorem in this file. A test (#guard) is an evaluation made while the file compiles;
the compile fails if the result moves; it leaves no theorem. Fmt/M1SpecKnown.lean states, and
Fmt/M1SpecKnownProofs.lean proves, that the description read off the bytes that Lean spells at these
sizes is the one at which the runs are judged; that these bytes are the programs' is the test named
above, not a theorem.

4. What is not claimed. Nothing on a proof file: that is tested in other files.
-/
import Fmt.M1ParOf

namespace Fmt
namespace M1

namespace Theirs

/-- the parameter bytes at full parameters, as they came -/
def full : ByteArray := unhex ""
/-- the parameter bytes at tiny parameters, as they came -/
def tiny : ByteArray := unhex ""
/-- the one public file at full parameters; `pubTiny`, below, is the one at tiny parameters -/
def pubFull : ByteArray := unhex ""
def pubTiny : ByteArray := unhex ""

/-- the sizes at tiny parameters -/
def tinyPar' : Par where
  fld := .two
  K := 4
  stages := [⟨⟨7, 16, 3⟩, ⟨7, 16, 3⟩, 2, 0, 3⟩,
    ⟨⟨8, 8, 3⟩, ⟨8, 32, 3⟩, 1, 1, 3⟩, ⟨⟨9, 8, 5⟩, ⟨9, 128, 5⟩, 0, 0, 5⟩]
  work := 6

end Theirs

#guard Theirs.full.size = 3765 && Theirs.tiny.size = 2949 && Theirs.pubFull.size = 180 && Theirs.pubTiny.size = 229
-- every part of the check holds at the programs' bytes, at both sets of parameters
#guard check Theirs.full = allHold
#guard check Theirs.tiny = allHold
-- the sizes that are read
#guard ((paramsRead Theirs.full).bind parOf).any fun P => samePar P toyPar
#guard ((paramsRead Theirs.tiny).bind parOf).any fun P => samePar P Theirs.tinyPar'
-- the programs' bytes, spelled by Lean from the sizes and the public file alone
#guard (specOf toyPar (configDigest [Theirs.pubFull]) (itemsOf toyPar)).params = Theirs.full
#guard (specOf Theirs.tinyPar' (configDigest [Theirs.pubTiny]) (itemsOf Theirs.tinyPar')).params = Theirs.tiny
-- the public files of the programs, spelled by Lean from the sizes, the first line and the target
-- (R12.7)
#guard publicFile "run product" 128 toyPar = Theirs.pubFull
#guard publicFile "run product, tiny edition: a test size that reaches no security level" 10 Theirs.tinyPar' = Theirs.pubTiny
-- the values that the programs print: the configuration's digest, the parameters' digest; with the
-- programs' random bytes, the label 1 and the empty statement: the run's value and the chain's
-- first value: at full parameters; at tiny parameters
#guard hex (configDigest [Theirs.pubFull]) = "4e8fe788c6b02284230e8019989ffe6cc53f71b438a71c75c588ed0c6999dc93"
#guard hex (paramsDigest Theirs.full) = "a0cecb8c51f49dd4fb30aa83c9ecbbd5a60d8ee2eadb517becff542160d050c7"
#guard ((startChain 1 (paramsDigest Theirs.full) ByteArray.empty (some (unhex "0e5a9714c77ff0ba731c0fd84eaa05d59e99776b4bf52de8fa3030d1dd90b07a"))).map fun c => (c.run.map hex, hex c.rho0)) = some (some "c3040ce5497effe933f3ab23067028c2e73f2f07a47ea90ae50ce20fee8560a1", "8438562914e72ea12a3280d50de4efa1370e950c7d4f7d4346521b3ad84b263c")
#guard hex (configDigest [Theirs.pubTiny]) = "17bbf2135e917055958937b0fde41e72baaf4cfd37f9c049829e28bb75557f3e"
#guard hex (paramsDigest Theirs.tiny) = "26d712eca24f846337fa037bfbe0cf44fb9e9922d3ace3dd4e0f37285835f76b"
#guard ((startChain 1 (paramsDigest Theirs.tiny) ByteArray.empty (some (unhex "2f7ae1e015fbf18838aad498fa01aaa9e703c535e4e3e3a0fd6dbb77b6124b1b"))).map fun c => (c.run.map hex, hex c.rho0)) = some (some "7b7c14d7e6a420e498fb2482d1bf4afb9f7a9c4302c974dcb3bbfcc52be57e63", "1a9f1184480f47bb9328bb6fbff82a22e987aa8399def17dc360fc7c92723e96")
-- the stages' words at full and at tiny parameters; the item of the codes at full parameters alone
#guard (itemsOf toyPar).lookup nameStages = some (stagesBytes [(0, 0, 2, 0), (1, 1, 1, 1), (1, 2, 0, 0)])
#guard (itemsOf Theirs.tinyPar').lookup nameStages = some (stagesBytes [(0, 0, 2, 0), (1, 1, 1, 1), (2, 2, 0, 0)])
#guard (itemsOf toyPar).lookup nameCodes = some (codesBytes [⟨15, 4096, 73⟩, ⟨14, 2048, 74⟩] [⟨15, 32, 73⟩, ⟨14, 256, 74⟩, ⟨14, 1024, 74⟩])

-- that the tests can fail: one bit of the bytes flipped: byte 165, the lowest byte of the first
-- entry's word of drawn elements: 17 for 16: the bytes are still read and spelled again, the sizes
-- are still read, but the plan no longer fits, at entry 0; and the digest is another
#guard byteAt Theirs.full 165 = 16 && byteAt (flipAt Theirs.full 165) 165 = 17
#guard check (flipAt Theirs.full 165) = { allHold with plan := false }
#guard ((paramsRead (flipAt Theirs.full 165)).bind fun p => (parOf p).map fun P => firstOff p P) = some (some 0)
#guard hex (paramsDigest (flipAt Theirs.full 165)) != "a0cecb8c51f49dd4fb30aa83c9ecbbd5a60d8ee2eadb517becff542160d050c7"

end M1
end Fmt
